Function Calling: Giving the LLM Hands to Call the Code

Yesterday, its about forcing the LLM to return strict, predictable JSON structures using schemas.
Today, I am taking the ultimate step in LLM orchestration, giving the LLM tools. By exposing the native C# methods as JSON schemas, the LLM can explicitly choose to call real code on my server to pull live data or take real-world actions.

LLMs cannot look up live system states, query databases, or check the current time on their own—they are frozen in their training cutoff data. 
**Function Calling** fixes this. Instead of trying to pass every piece of system data into the prompt upfront, I provide the LLM with a list of "Tools" (descriptions of my C# methods). When the user asks a question requiring external data, the LLM stops generating text and responds with a specific request: *"Please execute `GetIncidentDetails` with `id: 404` and give me the result."* My app runs the native C# code, feeds the return value back to the LLM, and the LLM synthesizes the final human-readable response.

The 3-Step Flow:
1. **Declare the C# Tool:** Define a clear, native C# method with detailed descriptions so the LLM understands exactly *when* and *how* to use it.
2. **Register the Tool:** Wrap the method inside `AIFunctionFactory.Create` and attach it to the `ChatOptions.Tools` collection.
3. **The Invocation Loop:** Send the message. If the LLM requests a tool call, execute the local C# function, append the execution results to the chat history array, and send it back to the LLM to finish its thought.

The Code Blueprint:
Using the standard `Microsoft.Extensions.AI` framework, wire up a live plugin tool seamlessly:

```csharp
using System.ComponentModel;
using Microsoft.Extensions.AI;
using OllamaSharp;

IChatClient client = new OllamaApiClient(new Uri("http://localhost:11434"), "llama3");

// 1. DECLARE THE C# TOOL: Document parameters clearly so the LLM parses correctly
[Description("Gets the deployment status and active error logs for a specific server node.")]
public string GetNodeStatus([Description("The identifier of the node, e.g., node-3")] string nodeId)
{
    // Real code execution happens here (DB lookups, API calls, etc.)
    if (nodeId == "node-3") 
        return "CRITICAL: Disk space exceeded (99% capacity). 43 timeout errors logged.";
    return "Node operational. Healthy status.";
}

// 2. REGISTER THE TOOL: Turn the method into an AI-readable function
var systemTools = new List<AIFunction>
{
    AIFunctionFactory.Create(GetNodeStatus)
};

var options = new ChatOptions { Tools = systemTools };

// 3. THE INVOCATION LOOP: Send query and let the client handle tool resolution
var history = new List<ChatMessage> { new ChatMessage(ChatRole.User, "Is node-3 failing right now?") };

var response = await client.CompleteAsync(history, options);

// Check if the LLM chose to call our code
if (response.Message.Contents.OfType<AIFunctionCallContent>().Any())
{
    foreach (var call in response.Message.Contents.OfType<AIFunctionCallContent>())
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"[LLM triggered tool: {call.Name} with args: {call.Arguments["nodeId"]}]");
        
        // Execute local C# function code
        object result = await call.InvokeAsync(systemTools);
        
        // Feed the factual results straight back into the conversation history
        history.Add(response.Message); 
        history.Add(new ChatMessage(ChatRole.Tool, result.ToString()) { AuthorName = call.Name });
    }

    // Send the completed ledger back to the LLM for final natural response generation
    var finalResponse = await client.CompleteAsync(history, options);
    Console.WriteLine($"\nAI response: {finalResponse.Message.Text}");
}

Key Takeaways:
The LLM Does Not Run the Code: The LLM does not execute anything securely or unsecurely on your server. It merely sends a text request saying: "Please run function X with parameter Y." Your application maintains absolute control over whether it actually invokes that method or blocks it.
Descriptions are the Code: The strings inside [Description] attributes are compile-time data that act as explicit prompt engineering instructions for the model's tool selection router. Bad descriptions lead to the LLM executing the wrong tools or passing malformed arguments.
The Two-Turn Conversation: A single function call requires a minimum of two round-trips to the LLM. Turn 1 determines which tool to call. Turn 2 summarizes the raw result returned from your code into a conversational response.
